+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `rune`
+
+Property-based tests were added to the `rune` crate using `hegeltest` (imported
+as `hegel`). All tests run under the project's CI configuration:
+`cargo test -p rune --all-features` (note: plain `cargo test -p rune --lib`
+does not compile even without these changes, because
+`src/tests/workspace/mod.rs` requires the `workspace` feature; CI always uses
+`--all-features`).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+12 properties (plus 2 pinned known-failure tests and 1 ignored abort
+reproducer), added to the existing test files they belong to:
+
+| # | Test | File | Property and evidence |
+|---|------|------|-----------------------|
+| 1 | `compile_never_panics_on_arbitrary_text` | `crates/rune/src/tests/compiler_general.rs` | Lexing/parsing/compiling arbitrary unicode text (both `text()` and raw-codepoint strings, in script and module mode) returns `Ok`/`Err` and never panics. Evidence: `prepare(...).build()` returns `Result<Unit, BuildError>` — the classic parser no-panic contract. |
+| 2 | `compile_never_panics_on_token_soup` | same | Same no-panic contract on "script-shaped" input: random sequences drawn from a vocabulary of real Rune keywords, operators, literals, comments and idents. Reaches much deeper into the parser than uniform noise. |
+| 3 | `nested_expressions_compile_without_crashing` | same | Nested `(`, `[`, `{`, `-(`, `#{a:`, `f(` expressions up to depth 20 compile or error without crashing the process. Depth is capped because deeper input aborts the process (bug 3 below). |
+| 4 | `arithmetic_matches_checked_i64_reference` | `crates/rune/src/tests/vm_arithmetic.rs` | Generated `+ - * / %`/unary-`-` expression trees over full-range `i64` literals evaluate to exactly what Rust's checked `i64` arithmetic computes; checked failures surface as the documented `VmErrorKind`s (`Overflow`/`Underflow`/`DivideByZero`). Evidence: `runtime/vm/ops.rs` maps each operator to `checked_*` with a specific error kind; existing `error_test!` cases in the same file assert single instances of this. |
+| 5 | `shift_ops_match_checked_reference` | same | `<<`/`>>` agree with `u32::try_from(shift)` + `checked_shl/shr` (evidence: `Vm::op_shift`, `runtime/vm.rs:1642`); invalid shift amounts always error (exact kind depends on evaluation path — see "Unsound properties" below). |
+| 6 | `const_eval_matches_runtime_eval` | `crates/rune/src/tests/vm_const_exprs.rs` | Differential: `const VALUE = <expr>; VALUE` (compile-time IR interpreter, `compile/ir/eval.rs`) equals `<expr>` evaluated by the VM, for non-overflowing `+ - * /` expressions. Overflowing expressions are excluded and pinned as bug 2. |
+| 7 | `integer_literal_roundtrip_all_bases` | `crates/rune/src/tests/literals.rs` | Any `i64` rendered as a decimal/hex/octal/binary literal, optionally with `_` separators and an `i64` suffix, evaluates back to itself — including `i64::MIN` via the negative-literal special case in `hir/lowering.rs`. Evidence: existing `number_literals_oob` and `test_number_literals` unit tests encode instances of this. |
+| 8 | `float_literal_roundtrip` | same | Any finite `f64` rendered with Rust's shortest-roundtrip formatting evaluates to bit-identical `f64` (exponents, subnormals, `-0.0`). Evidence: lexer exponent support (`parse/lexer.rs:217`), `compile/num.rs::from_float`. |
+| 9 | `string_literal_roundtrip` | same | Any string, escaped into a double-quoted literal (either minimally or entirely as `\u{..}` escapes), evaluates back to the original. Evidence: `ast/unescape.rs` escape set. |
+| 10 | `char_literal_roundtrip` | same | Any `char` (controls escaped, per the lexer's rejection of raw control characters) roundtrips through a char literal. |
+| 11 | `template_int_formatting_matches_rust` | `crates/rune/src/tests/vm_general.rs` | `` `${n}` `` equals Rust's `n.to_string()` for all `i64` (runtime integer formatting goes through `itoa`, which must agree with `std`). |
+| 12 | `budget_halts_infinite_loops` | same | For any budget (1..=100 000) and any of four never-terminating loop shapes, `budget::with(budget, || vm.call(..)).call()` returns an error instead of hanging, panicking, or completing. Evidence: `runtime/budget.rs` docs; `VmHalt::Limited` in `Vm::run`. |
+| 13 | `value_roundtrip_preserves_primitives` | `crates/rune/tests/derive_from_value.rs` | `from_value(to_value(x)) == x` for `i64`, `u64`, `f64` (bit-exact, incl. NaN), `bool`, `char` over their full domains. |
+| 14 | `value_roundtrip_preserves_compound_values` | same | Same roundtrip for `String`, `Vec<i64>`, `HashMap<String, i64>`, `Option<i64>`, and `(i64, String, bool)`. |
+
+Liveness was verified by temporarily inverting property 7's assertion: hegel
+reported and fully shrank the counterexample (`n = 0`, decimal). An
+exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on every property) found no failures
+beyond the two pinned bugs; the settings were then reverted to the defaults.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test -p rune --all-features --lib --test derive_from_value`:
+
+```
+364 passed; 2 failed; 1 ignored
+```
+
+- All 12 properties pass at the default 100 cases and at 1000 cases.
+- The 2 failures are the deterministic KNOWN-FAILURE pins for bugs 1 and 2
+  below (their generators are pinned with `generators::just(...)`, so they
+  fail identically on every run).
+- The 1 ignored test is the process-abort reproducer for bug 3 (running it
+  takes the whole test process down, so it must stay `#[ignore]`d).
+- All pre-existing tests still pass.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. `Vm::op_neg` uses unchecked negation (panic in debug, silent wrap in release)
+
+- **Minimal counterexample:** the script `let a = -9223372036854775808; -a`
+- **Code path:** `crates/rune/src/runtime/vm.rs:1377` —
+  `Inline::Signed(value) => Some(Inline::Signed(-value))` in `op_neg`.
+- **Observed:** panics with `attempt to negate with overflow` at
+  `vm.rs:1377:58` in debug builds; in release it wraps silently back to
+  `i64::MIN`.
+- **Expected:** every other arithmetic operator uses `checked_*` and reports
+  a `VmErrorKind` (e.g. `0 - a` on the same value correctly raises
+  `Underflow`). Negation should raise `Overflow` like `checked_neg` implies.
+- **Pinned by:** `tests::vm_arithmetic::negating_i64_min_is_not_checked`
+  (deterministically failing). Excluded from property 4 with a comment
+  referencing the pin.
+
+### 2. Const evaluation uses unchecked arithmetic (compiler panic in debug, silent wrap in release)
+
+- **Minimal counterexample:** the script
+  `const VALUE = 9223372036854775807 + 1; VALUE`
+- **Code path:** `crates/rune/src/compile/ir/eval.rs:84` — `a.add(b)` in
+  `eval_ir_binary`; the same match arm uses unchecked `sub`, `mul`, `shl`,
+  and `shr` (only `Div` is checked). E.g. `const VALUE = 1 << 64;` hits the
+  `shl` variant.
+- **Observed:** compiling (not running) the script panics with
+  `attempt to add with overflow` at `ir/eval.rs:84:57` in debug builds; in
+  release the constant silently wraps.
+- **Expected:** the identical expression without `const` raises
+  `VmErrorKind::Overflow` at runtime; const evaluation should report an
+  equivalent compile error (as it already does for division by zero).
+- **Pinned by:** `tests::vm_const_exprs::const_eval_overflow_panics_the_compiler`
+  (deterministically failing). Property 6 excludes overflowing expressions
+  with a comment referencing the pin.
+
+### 3. Deeply nested expressions abort the process with a stack overflow
+
+- **Minimal counterexample:** `-(-(-(...(0)...)))` at depth 40, or
+  `(((...0...)))` at depth 75, compiled on a default 2 MiB Rust test thread
+  (debug build). A larger stack moves the threshold but cannot remove it,
+  since there is no depth limit.
+- **Code path:** the recursive-descent parser/HIR lowering/assembler recurse
+  per nesting level; no recursion guard or depth limit was found in
+  `crates/rune/src/parse/parser.rs`.
+- **Observed:** `fatal runtime error: stack overflow, aborting` — SIGABRT,
+  killing the whole test process; no `Err` is ever returned.
+- **Expected:** an error like rustc's `E0602`-style recursion limit, or at
+  least a non-aborting failure. (This matches the class of bug previously
+  found in rhai's expression parser.)
+- **Pinned by:** `tests::compiler_general::deep_nesting_stack_overflow_reproducer`,
+  kept `#[ignore]`d per the process-abort protocol; property 3 is capped at
+  depth 20 with a comment.
+
+### Minor observations (not asserted as failures)
+
+- `%` in a const context fails with "op not supported yet"
+  (`ast::BinOp::Rem` has no `IrBinaryOp` counterpart) while `+ - * /` work —
+  surprising but an explicit, documented-by-error unimplemented feature.
+- Raw control characters (including DEL and the C1 range) inside a *char*
+  literal are rejected with the misleading error "Unterminated character
+  literal" (`parse/lexer.rs:309`), even when the literal is perfectly
+  terminated. Strings accept the same characters raw.
+- The error kind for an invalid shift amount depends on the evaluation path:
+  the `Shift` instruction reports `Overflow`/`Underflow` (e.g. `1 << 64`),
+  while a negative amount such as `0 >> -134217384` surfaces as
+  `ValueToIntegerCoercionError` through the protocol-call path.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Exact error kinds for shift failures** — first version of property 5
+  asserted `Overflow`/`Underflow` for every invalid shift amount; negative
+  amounts actually produce `ValueToIntegerCoercionError` via a different code
+  path (see observation above). Weakened to "must be a VM error".
+- **Char-literal roundtrip with raw control characters** — the lexer rejects
+  raw `is_control()` characters in char literals by design; the generator now
+  escapes them (kept, with the misleading error message noted above).
+- **Const/runtime differential over `%` and unary `-`** — both are
+  unsupported in const contexts (explicit "op not supported yet" /
+  "not const" errors), so they were removed from the const differential's
+  operator set rather than treated as disagreements.
+- A float-formatting differential (`` `${f}` `` vs Rust's `Display`) was
+  considered and not written: the runtime formats floats through `ryu`, whose
+  output legitimately differs from `std`'s `Display` for large exponents, so
+  there is no sound cross-oracle.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The **process-abort protocol** (bounded sibling property + `#[ignore]`d
+  reproducer + prominent documentation) applied directly to bug 3 and worked
+  well. Probing the abort threshold before committing a depth bound was
+  essential — the initial "safe" guess of 200 was itself aborting.
+- The **builder form** (`Hegel::new(|tc| ...)`) was load-bearing here:
+  `Context::with_default_modules()` is too expensive to rebuild per case, and
+  the free-function `#[hegel::test]` form cannot borrow a shared fixture.
+  Worth cross-referencing from the SKILL.md workflow, not just the Rust
+  reference.
+- Reference gotcha 12 (name collisions in existing test files) could mention
+  **prelude shadowing**: this crate's test prelude glob-imports its own
+  `Box`/`String`/`Vec` (`rune_alloc` types), so `Box::new(...)` in test code
+  fails with a confusing E0599 (`no function named `new` for
+  `rune_alloc::Box``). An explicit `use rust_alloc::boxed::Box;` fixes it.
+- The lack of a **global case-count override** (gotcha 13) made the 10x
+  exploratory pass a mechanical sed-edit-then-revert across six files; a
+  `HEGEL_TEST_CASES` env var would remove the main friction in the workflow.
+- No `.hegel/` failure database ever appeared in this environment despite
+  multiple recorded failures (likely CI auto-detection disabling it). The
+  `.gitignore` entry was added anyway per the reference; a note on how to
+  check whether the DB is active would help debugging replay surprises.
+- The advice to read existing unit tests first paid off twice: the existing
+  `error_test!` macros gave the exact expected `VmErrorKind` mapping for
+  property 4, and `number_literals_oob` justified including `i64::MIN` in the
+  literal roundtrip.
